---
import FirstRunTable from "../FirstRunTable.astro";
import Pipeline from "./Pipeline.astro";
import Voicemail from "./Voicemail.astro";
import { reported } from "../../lib/first-run";
import { bounds, story } from "../../lib/coverage";
import { noScore, pipeline, rileyAsk, spell } from "../../lib/cases";

const kickPlan = [
  `Download the schedule page from each of the ${spell(pipeline.asked)} school sites, and the box scores Alder Ridge's schedule links.`,
  "Parse each results table into rows: date, opponent, score.",
  "If a page fails to load, retry up to three times, then log a warning with the school's name and continue so the run finishes.",
  "Read each match once, from the home team's page, so none counts twice.",
  "Compute played, goal difference and points, then sort and write the table.",
  "Print a summary: pages fetched, matches found, errors.",
];

const openings = [
  { gap: "How many matches to expect", kick: "Report the number found.", then: `${story.counted}, with nothing to compare it to.`, href: "#case-dunmore", where: "Case 1" },
  { gap: "Which page to read each match from", kick: "The host's page, once, so no match counts twice.", then: `One lost page removed ${spell(bounds.unseen)} matches. Case 2 counts what else it bent.`, href: "#case-hartwell", where: "Case 2" },
  { gap: "What to do with a final that has no score", kick: "Nothing was stated. A row without a score is not counted.", then: `${noScore.home} and ${noScore.away} are each one match short.`, href: "#case-cedar-grove", where: "Case 3" },
  { gap: "What to do when a site is silent", kick: "Retry three times, log a warning, carry on.", then: "The run finishes without Dunmore.", href: "#decision", where: "The decision" },
  { gap: "What the report must say", kick: "Pages fetched, matches found, errors.", then: "“All eight school sites, no errors.”", href: "#rewind", where: "Rewind" },
];

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const film = (name: string) => `${base}film/${name}`;

const filmScript = [
  "You want a league table for eight schools. The results are out there, spread across eight websites, on pages made for people to read.",
  "A scraper is a program that downloads those pages and pulls rows out of them. The simplest one is a single loop.",
  "For each site: download the page, find the results table, read each row, and add it to the standings. On a good day, it works.",
  "Then one site doesn't answer. The loop waits, gives up, and the whole run fails, taking the finished sites down with it.",
  "So downloading becomes its own part: scrape. It works from a list of addresses, checks the answer each site sends back, waits, and tries again. When a site stays silent, scrape has to do something about it, and what it should do is a real decision. Hold on to that.",
  "A month later you find a bug. The parser swapped home and away scores. You fix it and go back for the pages, but the sites have moved on, and some of last month's pages are gone.",
  "So the next part is store. Save every page exactly as it arrived, before any of your code reads it. Now a bug is cheap. Fix the parser and run it again on the saved pages, without asking the web for anything.",
  "The third break is quieter. A site is redesigned, and the results are no longer the first table on the page. The loop reads the wrong table, and nothing complains.",
  "So parse becomes a part of its own. It finds a table by what the table says it is, and hands on rows in one fixed shape: date, opponent, result.",
  "Now put the rows together. A match between two schools is listed on both schools' pages. Add up every row, and every match counts twice.",
  "So build is where rows become matches. It decides which copy of each match to keep, and only then works out the standings.",
  "Last, the run ends and hands you a table. Did every site answer? How many rows came in? Were there errors? The table can't say.",
  "So report writes down what the run did: the pages it fetched, the rows it found, and the errors it hit.",
  "Line the parts up, and you have a pipeline. Each stage takes what the last one made and hands on something new. Each can be checked on its own, and run again without starting over.",
  "Now look at what that report counts. Every line in it is about what the run did. None of it says what the run should have found. That is the question this module is about.",
];

const learnPrompt =`I'm a data science student learning how data is collected from websites. Teach me what a web scraper is and how it becomes a data pipeline. Use one running example throughout: collecting a season of match results from eight school athletics sites to build a league table.

1. Walk me through the parts a scraper needs: a list of what to fetch, fetching (requests, status codes, retries, rate limits, robots.txt), storing the raw pages before parsing them, parsing HTML tables into rows, building the output, and reporting on the run. For each part, tell me why it exists and what goes wrong without it.
2. Show how those parts chain into a pipeline: what each stage takes in and what it hands to the next.
3. For each stage, name one way it can lose data without raising an error.
4. Sketch the stages as separate Python functions, working on a saved HTML string, not a live site.
5. End with three questions that check my understanding. Wait for my answers before you give yours.`;

const choices = [
  ["stop", "Stop the run"],
  ["skip", "Skip it and carry on"],
  ["fill", "Fill in 0-0"],
  ["record", "Record the gap"],
];
---

<section id="setup">
  <h2>Setup: one sentence, one Friday</h2>
  <p>Here is the film's Friday again, slowly, as a problem with a cause.</p>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>1</span>The ask</h3>
  <blockquote class="cv-ask">
    <p class="cv-who">Riley, to Kick <span>4:12</span></p>
    <p>{rileyAsk}</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>It names the season, the sport, the sources and the product. It reads as complete.</p>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>2</span>What Kick built</h3>
  <p>Kick built a scraper, a program that downloads pages made for people and pulls rows out of them. This three-minute film builds one from a single loop, breaking it five times on the way.</p>
  <figure class="cv-film">
    <video controls playsinline preload="metadata" poster={film("one-loop-to-a-pipeline.jpg")} aria-label="One Loop to a Pipeline, a three-minute animated explainer">
      <source src={film("one-loop-to-a-pipeline.mp4")} type="video/mp4" />
      <track kind="captions" src={film("one-loop-to-a-pipeline.vtt")} srclang="en" label="English" default />
      <track kind="chapters" src={film("one-loop-to-a-pipeline-chapters.vtt")} srclang="en" label="Chapters" />
    </video>
    <figcaption>One Loop to a Pipeline. Schools are lettered and the numbers are illustrative.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <details class="cv-more">
    <summary>Read the film's narration</summary>
    {filmScript.map((x) => <p>{x}</p>)}
  </details>
  <p>Kick's pipeline has the same five stages. Here they are on Friday, with what left each one.</p>
  <Pipeline />
  <details class="cv-more">
    <summary>Kick's plan, in its own words</summary>
    <ol class="cv-plan">{kickPlan.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ol>
  </details>
  <p>A pipeline can be asked three questions.</p>
  <ul class="cv-three">
    <li class="is-here"><b>Is everything here?</b><span><strong>Completeness</strong>, measured as coverage. This module.</span></li>
    <li><b>Is each value in a usable form?</b><span>Cleansing. <a href="../04-parsing/">Modules 4</a> and <a href="../05-identity/">5</a>.</span></li>
    <li><b>Does each record obey the rules?</b><span>Validation. <a href="../06-contracts/">Modules 6</a> and <a href="../07-gates/">7</a>.</span></li>
  </ul>
  <p>All {story.counted} of Kick's matches are real and correctly read, so cleansing and validation would both pass this table. A missing record has nothing to fail.</p>
  <div class="cv-prompt cv-learn">
    <p class="cv-prompt-head">Learn more with AI</p>
    <p class="cv-prompt-note">New to scrapers? Paste this into any AI assistant for a short tutorial, then come back for the cases.</p>
    <p data-copy-text tabindex="0">{learnPrompt}</p>
    <button type="button" data-copy>Copy the prompt</button>
  </div>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>3</span>What the ask left open</h3>
  <p>Kick filled five openings in the sentence with reasonable choices, and mentioned none. Open them one at a time.</p>
  <div class="cv-open">
    {openings.map((o) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{o.gap}</summary>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Kick chose</dt><dd>{o.kick}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Then</dt><dd>{o.then}</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Covered in</dt><dd data-rail-hint={o.href.slice(1)}>{o.where}</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </details>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>4</span>What Riley saw</h3>
  <p>At 4:52 this was everything on Riley's screen.</p>
  <div class="cv-say">
    <p class="cv-who">Kick, the coding agent</p>
    <p>Done. All eight school sites, {story.counted} conference matches, no errors. Ready to publish.</p>
  </div>
  <FirstRunTable rows={reported} caption="The table Kick delivered" />
  <div class="cv-commit" data-commit>
    <label class="cv-write" for="cv-looks">What here looks wrong to you? Nothing is marked.</label>
    <textarea id="cv-looks" rows="2" data-save="looks-wrong"></textarea>
    <button type="button" disabled>Lock it in and read on</button>
    <p class="cv-once" data-needs-note>Write at least one sentence.</p>
    <div class="cv-reveal">
      <p>Eight rows, sorted, plausible numbers, no blanks. Dunmore on {spell(bounds.played)} played looks wrong only to someone who already knows the season is longer.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>5</span>How it surfaced</h3>
  <p>Riley published at 4:58. The table's readers found the problem. Riley had no way to.</p>
  <div class="cv-calls">
    <Voicemail who="dolan" caller="Coach Dolan, Hartwell" time="5:41" line="We went into Dunmore October fifteenth and put seven on 'em. Seven-nothing. You've got us fourth." />
    <Voicemail who="dunmore" caller="Dunmore athletics" time="5:44" line="You've got our men at six conference games. We've played thirteen." />
    <Voicemail who="cedar" caller="Cedar Grove men's soccer" time="from the bus" line="September twenty-first, us and St. Lambert. That game happened. It's not in the standings." />
  </div>

  <h3 class="cv-step"><span>6</span>The problem, stated</h3>
  <p class="cv-lede">Kick's report described its program. Riley needed a report on the data. The records are clean and valid, and some are missing.</p>
  <div class="cv-say cv-yours" data-first hidden>
    <p class="cv-who">Your question from the film</p>
    <p data-first-text></p>
  </div>
  <fieldset class="cv-choice" data-gate>
    <legend>Dunmore's site does not answer. What should Kick do?</legend>
    {choices.map(([value, label]) => (
      <label><input type="radio" name="cv-decision" value={value} data-save="decision" /><span>{label}</span></label>
    ))}
  </fieldset>
  <p class="cv-once">Nothing is marked. You come back to it after the three cases.</p>
  <p class="cv-can">Next comes the idea behind all three calls, then one case per call. Then you can:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say how many items a collection should hold, before it runs and from outside it</li>
    <li>trace a gap to the block it removes, and bound what it touches</li>
    <li>tell empty, unknown and never asked apart, and say what each lets you claim</li>
    <li>choose what a collector does when a source is silent, and say when a run has succeeded</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="cv-next">First, the idea all three calls share.</p>
</section>
